{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1791981696542,"sessionID":"ses_ec592d9b9ffepF2Avmj7MbRoO1","part":{"id":"prt_13a6eb61e001Q5RMq1umTxcqA9","sessionID":"ses_ec592d9b9ffepF2Avmj7MbRoO1","messageID":"msg_13a6eb216001W1XgtW4l63TDtC","type":"step-start","snapshot":"bbe603e49c25e720b8643460b8f284cd976eb192"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1791981767505,"sessionID":"ses_ec592d9b9ffepF2Avmj7MbRoO1","part":{"id":"prt_13a6eb6240011HxF75VMHs4FOH","sessionID":"ses_ec592d9b9ffepF2Avmj7MbRoO1","messageID":"msg_13a6eb216001W1XgtW4l63TDtC","type":"text","text":"---\n\n# Requirements Elicitation Package\n\n## Understanding\n\n**Requested outcome:** The account service sends a password-reset message to a user within a bounded time after the user requests a password reset.\n\n**Proposed system boundary:** The account service — specifically its outbound notification path — at the point where it dispatches the reset message to an external delivery channel.\n\n**Source material:** Two supplied requirements (R1, R2) and a prose gap statement naming three missing behaviors.\n\n**Information sorting:**\n\n| Source statement | Kind |\n|---|---|\n| R1 — account service sends a \"reset message\" within 60 seconds | Behavioral obligation (candidate) |\n| R2 — account service sends a \"password-reset email\" within 1 minute | Behavioral obligation — likely duplicate of R1; medium differs slightly |\n| \"No behavior for an unavailable mail provider\" | Identified gap — failure-path obligation |\n| \"No behavior for repeated requests\" | Identified gap — boundary/lifecycle obligation |\n| \"No behavior for an expired reset link\" | Identified gap — state/lifecycle obligation |\n\n**Key observations before drafting:**\n\n1. **Duplicate or near-duplicate:** R1 (\"reset message\" / 60 seconds) and R2 (\"password-reset email\" / 1 minute) describe the same trigger, the same responsible system, and the same timing limit expressed in different units. They differ only in the word used for the outbound artifact. This is an **overlap / near-duplicate** finding. The intended behavior is ambiguous: are both terms referring to an email, or is \"reset message\" a broader channel (SMS, push, in-app)? This must be resolved before either can be marked ready.\n2. **Timing units are identical in magnitude** (60 s = 1 min) but inconsistently expressed.\n3. **Three gaps** are acknowledged by the submitter: unavailable mail provider, repeated requests, and expired reset link. None of these have requirements.\n\n---\n\n## Clarifying Questions\n\n**Q1 (Duplicate / channel scope — blocks R1 and R2):**\nR1 uses \"reset message\" and R2 uses \"password-reset email.\" Do both requirements describe the same delivery channel (email), making one redundant? Or does \"reset message\" intentionally cover additional channels (e.g., SMS, push)? If multiple channels are intended, is the 60-second deadline per channel or for all channels collectively?\n\n**Q2 (Timing — blocks R1 and R2):**\nShould the 60-second / 1-minute deadline be expressed as a single canonical value and unit? Is the deadline measured from when the account service *receives* the user's request or from when it *begins processing* it?\n\n**Q3 (Mail-provider failure — blocks SR-A):**\nWhen the external mail provider is unavailable, what shall the account service do? Candidates include: retry with a defined schedule, queue the message for later delivery, return an error to the calling interface, or notify the user through an alternate channel. Which behavior is required, and what retry limit or timeout applies?\n\n**Q4 (Repeated requests — blocks SR-B):**\nWhen a user submits multiple password-reset requests before the first link is used or expires, shall the account service honor each independently, invalidate prior pending links, or reject subsequent requests within a window? If rate-limited, what is the limit and the response when exceeded?\n\n**Q5 (Reset-link expiry — blocks SR-C):**\nWhen a user attempts to use a reset link after it has expired, what shall the account service do? What expiry duration is intended?\n\n**Q6 (Audit / confirmation — optional):**\nIs the account service required to record an event (log entry, audit trail) when a reset message is dispatched, when delivery fails, or when a link is used or expires?\n\n---\n\n## Candidate Requirements\n\n### Candidate 1 (from R1)\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** Event-driven\n- **Requirement:** When a user requests a password reset, the account service shall dispatch a password-reset [message | email — see Q1] within 60 seconds of receiving the request.\n- **Source intent and rationale:** R1 asserts a bounded latency so users receive the reset credential promptly. The intended channel is unresolved.\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: Account service, outbound notification interface.\n  - Responsible system: Account service.\n  - Actor: User (interface — web form, API, etc. — not specified).\n  - Affected interfaces: Account service to external mail/messaging provider; response interface back to caller.\n  - Inputs and data definitions: Password-reset request for an identified user account. \"Reset message\" channel undefined (Q1 open). User identity lookup not defined in R1/R2.\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Trigger is user submitting a password-reset request. No precondition on account state (e.g., active, unblocked) is stated.\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — reset message dispatched within 60 seconds. Alternate and failure — undefined (Q3, Q4 open).\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: 60 seconds. Measurement start open (Q2). No tolerance stated.\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \"Reset message\" vs. \"password-reset email\" (Q1 open). Measurement start (Q2 open). Possible duplicate of Candidate 2.\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: Valid user account exists; password-reset request submitted to the account service.\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: Reset message received on the target delivery channel; timestamp of dispatch compared to timestamp of request receipt.\n  - Expected result: Reset message dispatched within 60 seconds of request receipt.\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass if dispatch ≤ 60 s from request receipt. Fail otherwise. \"Dispatch\" boundary (internal vs. provider handoff) is open (Q2).\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: No message sent (fail). Message sent after 60 s (fail). Provider unavailable (Q3 open). Repeated requests (Q4 open).\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: Timestamps at request receipt and at dispatch; account-service outbound log or provider delivery confirmation.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: No — the 60-second constraint requires timestamps at both the request-receipt boundary and the dispatch boundary.\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: Internal request-receipt timestamp and outbound dispatch timestamp both required to measure compliance.\n\n---\n\n### Candidate 2 (from R2)\n\n- **Status:** needs clarification\n- **Template:** Event-driven\n- **Requirement:** When a user requests a password reset, the account service shall dispatch a password-reset email within 60 seconds of receiving the request.\n- **Source intent and rationale:** R2 specifies email as the channel and states a 1-minute (= 60-second) limit. Likely describes the same behavior as R1 with a more specific channel term. Overlap with Candidate 1 is open pending Q1.\n- **Implementability contract**\n  - System boundary: Account service, outbound email interface.\n  - Responsible system: Account service.\n  - Actor: User.\n  - Affected interfaces: Account service to external email provider.\n  - Inputs and data definitions: Password-reset request for a user with a registered email address. Email address validity and lookup not defined.\n  - Preconditions, state, feature scope, and trigger: Same trigger as Candidate 1. No stated precondition on account state.\n  - Observable normal, alternate, and failure response: Normal — email dispatched within 60 seconds. Alternate and failure — undefined (Q3 open).\n  - Quantities, units, limits, timing, ordering, and tolerances: 60 seconds (1 minute). Measurement start open (Q2). No tolerance stated.\n  - Domain terms, dependencies, and unresolved decisions: \"Password-reset email\" more specific than R1's \"reset message\"; relationship open (Q1). Measurement start (Q2 open). Possible duplicate of Candidate 1.\n- **Verification contract**\n  - Setup, state, input data, and stimulus: Valid user account with registered email address; password-reset request submitted.\n  - Observable evidence or oracle: Password-reset email received in target inbox; dispatch/delivery timestamp compared to request timestamp.\n  - Expected result: Email dispatched within 60 seconds of request receipt.\n  - Pass/fail criteria, including scope, thresholds, units, and timing: Pass if dispatch ≤ 60 s. \"Dispatch\" vs. \"delivery\" boundary open (Q2).\n  - Boundary, negative, and failure cases: No email sent (fail). Email sent after deadline (fail). Email provider unavailable (Q3 open). User has no registered email address (undefined). Repeated requests (Q4 open).\n  - Required data, instrumentation, assessment, or external evidence: Request-receipt and dispatch timestamps; provider delivery log or test mailbox.\n  - Observable at the named boundary alone: No — same dual-timestamp constraint as Candidate 1.\n  - If no, internal evidence needed and rationale: Internal request-receipt timestamp and outbound dispatch timestamp both required.\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Supporting Requirements\n\n**SR-A — Mail-provider unavailability (Failure-path companion)**\nRelated to Candidates 1 and 2 (normal dispatch path). When the external mail provider is unavailable when the account service attempts dispatch, no response is defined. Without this, the 60-second obligation has no failure path and implementers will choose inconsistent behavior. Provisional sentence pending Q3:\n> If the external mail provider is unavailable when the account service attempts to dispatch a password-reset message, then the account service shall [retry on a defined schedule / queue for later delivery / return an error — to be determined by Q3].\n\n**SR-B — Repeated reset requests (Boundary companion)**\nRelated to Candidates 1 and 2 (trigger scope). When a user submits a second password-reset request before the first link is consumed or expires, no behavior is defined. Concurrent reset tokens create a security ambiguity. Provisional sentence pending Q4:\n> While a password-reset request is pending for an account, when a new password-reset request is received for the same account, the account service shall [invalidate the prior link / reject the new request / honor both — to be determined by Q4].\n\n**SR-C — Expired reset link (State/lifecycle companion)**\nRelated to Candidates 1 and 2 (lifecycle of the reset credential). When a user attempts to use a reset link after it has expired, no response is defined. The reset credential's lifecycle is incomplete without this. Provisional sentence pending Q5:\n> If a user submits an expired password-reset link, then the account service shall [return an error / redirect to a new-request flow — to be determined by Q5].\n\n**SR-D — Reset-link expiry duration (Required companion for SR-C)**\nRelated to SR-C. The duration for which a reset link remains valid is not stated in R1 or R2. Without it, SR-C cannot be verified and implementers will choose arbitrary values. Provisional sentence pending Q5:\n> The account service shall invalidate a password-reset link [N minutes/hours — to be determined by Q5] after it is dispatched.\n\n**SR-E — Audit event for dispatch (Optional consideration)**\nRelated to Candidates 1 and 2. Password-reset flows are a common vector for account-takeover attacks. Recording a timestamped event when a reset message is dispatched supports security monitoring and incident investigation. Not implied by R1 or R2; ask whether it is in scope (Q6).\n\n---\n\n## Consistency Findings\n\n### Finding 1 — Duplicate / Overlap: R1 and R2\n\n- **Affected items:** Candidate 1 (R1), Candidate 2 (R2).\n- **Condition:** Identical trigger (user requests a password reset), identical responsible system (account service), identical timing constraint (60 s = 1 min). They differ only in the label for the outbound artifact: \"reset message\" vs. \"password-reset email.\"\n- **Consequence:** If both terms denote the same channel, the requirements are near-identical duplicates that will diverge as they are maintained separately. If \"reset message\" is intentionally broader, the two requirements overlap on the email channel and may impose two independent obligations, risking double dispatch.\n- **Resolution options:**\n  1. Confirm both refer to email → consolidate into one requirement using \"password-reset email.\"\n  2. Confirm \"reset message\" covers multiple channels → scope each requirement to its channel, one per channel or one covering all configured channels.\n- **Blocks:** Both candidates remain `needs clarification` until Q1 is answered.\n\n### Finding 2 — Gap: No failure path for provider unavailability\n\n- **Affected items:** Candidates 1 and 2.\n- **Condition:** Both candidates require dispatch within 60 seconds, but no requirement exists for what happens if the external provider is unavailable during that window.\n- **Consequence:** An implementer cannot make a conforming choice. A verifier cannot determine pass or fail for a test with a simulated provider outage.\n- **Resolution options:** Add SR-A with the answer to Q3.\n\n### Finding 3 — Gap: No behavior for repeated reset requests\n\n- **Affected items:** Candidates 1 and 2.\n- **Condition:** The trigger does not specify whether it applies once per pending reset or on every invocation. Multiple valid reset tokens could coexist with undefined precedence.\n- **Consequence:** Two implementers could produce different security postures for the same trigger.\n- **Resolution options:** Add SR-B with the answer to Q4.\n\n### Finding 4 — Gap: No reset-link lifecycle behavior\n\n- **Affected items:** Candidates 1 and 2 (downstream of dispatch).\n- **Condition:** Neither requirement defines how long a dispatched reset link remains valid or what happens when an expired link is submitted.\n- **Consequence:** The reset flow is incomplete. A user who receives a link but delays acting has no defined outcome.\n- **Resolution options:** Add SR-C and SR-D with the answers to Q5.\n\n---\n\n## Assumptions\n\n**A1 (Provisional):** \"Reset message\" in R1 and \"password-reset email\" in R2 refer to the same delivery channel (email). Provisional pending Q1. If wrong, separate channel-scoped candidates are needed.\n\n**A2 (Provisional):** The 60-second and 1-minute constraints are identical in magnitude. Canonical unit going forward is 60 seconds. Measurement start is assumed to be receipt of the user's request at the account service boundary, pending Q2.\n\n**A3 (Provisional):** \"Dispatch\" means the account service has handed the message to the external provider's API or interface — not confirmed delivery to the recipient's inbox. This must be confirmed to close the verification contracts.\n\n---\n\n## Rationale and Context\n\nR1 and R2 appear to be independently authored statements of the same requirement — a bounded delivery latency for the password-reset credential. The inconsistent terminology (\"reset message\" vs. \"password-reset email\") is the primary source of ambiguity: it creates an unresolved overlap that could produce either a redundant duplicate or two conflicting obligations depending on intent.\n\nNeither statement addresses failure paths, which are essential for a security-sensitive flow. The submitter correctly identified the three gap areas (provider unavailability, repeated requests, expired links). None of them has a corresponding requirement, so the set is incomplete for implementation.\n\nThe most important immediate action is to resolve **Q1** (duplicate/channel scope) and then **Q3–Q5** (failure and lifecycle paths). **Q2** (measurement start) is needed to close the verification contracts for the two candidates once Q1 is resolved.\n\n---\n\n## Review Status\n\n| Item | Status | Blocking issue |\n|---|---|---|\n| Candidate 1 (R1) | **needs clarification** | Q1 (channel), Q2 (timing start), Q3 (failure path); Finding 1 |\n| Candidate 2 (R2) | **needs clarification** | Q1 (channel/duplicate), Q2 (timing start), Q3 (failure path); Finding 1 |\n| SR-A | suggestion — not a candidate | Pending Q3 |\n| SR-B | suggestion — not a candidate | Pending Q4 |\n| SR-C | suggestion — not a candidate | Pending Q5 |\n| SR-D | suggestion — not a candidate | Pending Q5 |\n| SR-E | optional consideration | Pending Q6 |\n| Finding 1 (duplicate/overlap) | open | Q1 must be answered |\n| Finding 2 (gap: provider failure) | open | SR-A not yet accepted |\n| Finding 3 (gap: repeated requests) | open | SR-B not yet accepted |\n| Finding 4 (gap: link lifecycle) | open | SR-C/SR-D not yet accepted |\n\n**No candidate is `ready for review`.** Both candidates remain `needs clarification` pending answers to Q1 and Q2 at minimum, and pending a decision on which gap-filling supporting requirements are in scope. The duplicate/overlap finding (Finding 1) must be resolved before either candidate can advance.\n\n---\n\nThe package has also been written to `artifacts/result.md`.","time":{"start":1791981696548,"end":1791981767505}}}
